Domain Name
A domain name is a unique and readable address used to access a website on the internet through the Domain Name System (DNS).

Maps a human-friendly name to an IP address.
Follows a hierarchical structure: subdomain, domain name, and TLD.
Registered and renewed through domain registrars.
Example: In www.example.com, www is the subdomain, example is the domain name, and .com is the top-level domain.
Browser Rendering
Browser rendering is the process by which a web browser converts webpage code into a visible and interactive page for the user.

The browser’s rendering engine parses HTML and CSS.
Builds the Document Object Model (DOM) from HTML.
Applies CSS rules to style the DOM elements.
Executes JavaScript to add dynamic behavior.
Displays the final rendered output to the user.
Rendering performance depends on browser, device, network speed, and page complexity.
Client-Side Rendering
Client-side rendering (CSR) is a web rendering approach where the browser builds and displays the webpage using resources provided by the server.

Rendering is handled entirely by the user’s browser.
Server delivers HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files.
JavaScript controls page updates and interactions.
Enables dynamic and interactive user experiences.
Commonly used in single-page applications (SPAs).
Server-Side Rendering
Server-side rendering (SSR) is a rendering approach where the server generates the complete HTML page and sends it to the browser.

Rendering occurs on the server.
Suitable for content-heavy or frequently updated websites.
Improves initial page load and SEO.
Commonly used in CMS platforms.
Inspect Command
The Inspect command is a browser tool that lets you view and modify a webpage’s HTML, CSS, and JavaScript, and is available in modern browsers like Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Microsoft Edge.

To use the inspect command, follow these steps:

Open your web browser and navigate to the webpage you want to inspect.
Right-click anywhere on the page and select "Inspect" from the context menu. Alternatively, you can use the keyboard shortcut Ctrl+Shift+I (Windows, Linux) or Cmd+Option+I (Mac).
The developer tools window will appear, showing you the HTML, CSS, and JavaScript of the page.
Use the various tabs and tools within the developer tools to inspect and manipulate the webpage as needed.
